A: Your test case seems to have a wrong assumption. The LastIndexOf() method returns the zero-based index of the last occurrence of a value in a collection, so it will return a value between 0 and Count - 1 when there are at least one occurrence. It will return -1 if the value is not found. 
So if the count of your list is 0(no element), the last index of a value won't change, its count will remain 0. But if there are at least one element in your list, the count won't decrease. Your assumption seems to suggest Count can decrease by 1 when a value is added, which is contradictory.
Thus, I would also suggest you take Count as the invariant, and any other properties that can help you prove your test cases.
Hope this would help solving your problem.

A: If I understand your question properly, your objective is to validate the contract of the LastIndexOf method, but it appears that you might be getting a little mixed up with what PEx is telling you to observe and assert on.
The LastIndexOf method is a contract of the ArrayList class, so your contract test should be looking at the ArrayList object for any behavior. Specifically, it should be testing:


*

*If you add an element to the list and ask for its last index, you should get back the index of the new element added to the list.

*If you add an element to the list and then delete it, but ask for the last index, you should still get the highest index (count - 1)


In your Pex method, you are observing the Count after the add and before the remove and checking that the Counts are the same. This seems to be doing the job for you, but here is a more explicit Pex method:
[PexMethod]
public void LastIndexOfContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
{
    // Assume that the list has been initialized
    Assume.True(arrList.Count >= 0);

    // Add the item
    arrList.Add(x);
    int indexOfAddedItem = arrList.Count - 1; // we've just added an item, so the last index should be one less than the count

    // Check the last index of the added item
    int lastIndexOfAddedItem = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);

    // Remove the item
    arrList.RemoveAt(indexOfAddedItem);

    // Check that the last index of the removed item (which was the same added item) is correctly updated
    Assert.AreEqual(lastIndexOfAddedItem, arrList.LastIndexOf(x));
}

This checks two things about ArrayList's LastIndexOf method:


*

*After adding an item, last index of that item should be the index of the last item, which is count - 1 because it's 0-based.

*Once an item is removed, the last index should be updated to reflect the removal.


Hope this makes more sense.
Cheers,
-M
