A: The issue was not in the LastIndexOf function itself but is related to the Pex method generation. When trying to execute Pex method for the ArrayList Add method which is a void method, Pex does not consider such methods to be part of the contract.

In a nutshell, Add is void method in .NET, but Pex is not equipped to handle void methods for method generation.
I guess the contract of Add was designed to only include methods that return some value, not void. 

Adding the int parameter to the Add method and then ignoring its return value fixed the issue that I had. I cannot explain why Add method works in practice returning that parameter but not when Pex tries to verify its contract. 
I hope this helps others who might face the same problem. 

Also I must thank @SergeL comment, which gave me the clue to proceed in the right direction. 
So, with Pex you can make assertions on public methods only. You can't make assertions on methods that do not return a value and do not include an [PexMethod] attribute.

A: I have faced a similar issue, and the problem is in Pex's analysis. In .NET, ArrayList.Add method does not participate in the contract analysis, because it's a void method.
Here are two solutions:


*

*Ignore void method Add when you perform the Pex run. That means you'll receive an incorrect result in PexTests, but your tests will not fail. This can be useful if you just want to perform regression tests. The downside is that the test will not check if Add method behaves as it is supposed to in the context of ArrayList.

*Change ArrayList to List<T> (assuming that your method has T as the input parameter type). List<T> does participate in the contract analysis and you may not have any issues.


Hope this helps.

A: The problem here isn't with Pex. ArrayList.Add method doesn't return anything, which means it doesn't satisfy the precondition to a method under test, which is the reason why you are getting failures. 

In the context of Pex testing, public methods are typically well covered, but it doesn't work with non-returning methods like the Add method for Collections.

Here are couple possible work arounds :


*

*Ignore the failing test scenario for add method as it's just for validation tests.

*Switch to use List instead of ArrayList, which is a more fully-fledged generic collection and hence would meet the precondition better.

*Manually create another test to check that the add method is working correctly.


Lastly, ensure you understand the role of ArrayList because it may not suit your needs due to its lack of generics, and you might have to refactor your code to work with List or another suitable generic collection.

