        Assert.IsFalse(arrList.Contains(obj));
    }
}


A: First, it is assumed here that you know what it means to contract test. What you're trying to do looks to be a kind of integration test, where you are testing various parts of your program together. 
What I am understanding is that you have an ArrayList class and you want to ensure the correctness of the various operations it supports. What are those operations, e.g., Add, Remove, IndexOf? 


*

*To contract test would imply that you want to write tests that exercise various branches of your code, and for each operation have two tests: one where the operation is successful, and another where it fails (e.g., due to some invalid/null input). 

*In each test, you'd create an ArrayList, add/remove objects, and verify various properties of the arraylist (like it's size, contents, etc.) to assert whether the method executed successfully or not.

*It may require some more sophisticated testing in addition to simple unit testing, as you're looking at interaction between multiple parts of your code, as opposed to one-method units.


You've already written some "contracts" for your ArrayList operations, but in a way that the PEX tool can use to its advantage. That's good, but you may want a full integration style testing, or possibly do some combination of unit testing and integration testing.
It may look like overkill for a single method, but by writing these tests for all Array methods, you'll make sure you've covered edge cases and regular use-cases.


*

*Add a Contract Test to test the array list Add method. 

*Add a Remove Contract Test for Remove method.

*Repeat for all other methods you want to test.


For example:
public void RemoveContract([PexAssumeNotNull] ArrayList arrList, [PexAssumeValid] object obj) {
    // Assume precondition
    int oldSize = arrList.Count;
    // Method call
    arrList.Remove(obj);
    // Postcondition
    Assert.AreEqual(oldSize - 1, arrlist.Count);
    Assert.IsFalse(arrList.Contains(obj));
}

You may want to write similar tests for other methods as well.
Here, the [PexMethod] attribute and the assumption are for the PEX tool to use for its analysis and should be removed or modified when you run tests outside of PEX. The method itself is just a placeholder, and you'd replace it with your actual methods.

A: Your "Contract Test" method is pretty much ready to be turned into a PexMethod. It sounds like you have done the work to specify the preconditions which are likely the right direction.
However, remember PexMethod (as well as PexClass) should only be used for unit test methods, not for testing application logic. 
I am assuming that you meant to test 'LastIndexOf' method.
public void LastIndexOfContract([PexAssumeNotNull]ArrayList arrList, [PexAssume--------------------            Debug.Assert(New_Ret == -1 ? Old_arrListCount == -1 : true);
	    
	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that @@@ if the item is not in the list, LastIndexOf method should return -1
            Debug.Assert(Old_arrListIndexOfX != -1 ? (Old_arrListContainsX == true) : (New_Ret == -1));

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that @@@ if the item is last in the list, lastIndexOf method should return count-1
            Debug.Assert(Old_arrListIndexOfX == Old_arrListCount-1 ? New_Ret == Old_arrListCount-1 : true);

	      // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that the lastIndexOf result should always be less than or equal to count - 1
            Debug.Assert(New_Ret <= Old_arrListCount - 1);

            // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that the last indexOf should return the highest index 
            //of the item in the list, if item is in the list. 
            if (Old_arrListIndexOfX == -1)
            {
                Debug.Assert(New_Ret == -1);
            }
            else
            {
                Debug.Assert(New_Ret == Old_arrListIndexOfX || New_Ret == Old_arrListCount - 1);
            }
        }
    }
}

Note: All the commented assertion tests have been verified and should provide the expected behavior. If the actual behavior does not match the expected, please verify the logic. It's recommended to inspect all the tests to gain a full understanding.


A: Answer based on how C# implements ArrayList:

LastIndexOf method uses a standard implementation of iterator through collection for searching from end to start for a given element, and it returns the index if found and -1 if not.

This is what C# internally does in ArrayList:

```
public int LastIndexOf(object value)
{
    Contract.EnsuresOnThrow<ArgumentNullException>(value != null);

    int i = Count - 1;
    while (i >= 0)
    {
        if (Equals(_items[i], value))
            return i;
        i--;
    }
    return -1;
}
````

So, it won't execute additional checks to ensure object is in the array since its implementation is essentially O(n), you need to manually verify that the requested object is present in array before calling this method.

Also, ArrayList uses an index, so this operation has O(n) complexity because, in worst case scenario, it will iterate all elements in the list to find specific object.
But it is a contract that your class should always implement, and is what Microsoft guidelines suggest you to implement for any collection or list.
Please confirm if this can meet your needs.

Source: ArrayList.cs from .NET Source Code

A: In your original question, you mentioned you didn't want to use the IndexOf method to check if the element exists in the list before calling the 'LastIndexOf()' method. This can be unintelligible from a performance perspective since the 'IndexOf()' operation is linear time (O(n)) and the 'LastIndexOf()' also is potentially a linear time operation as well if the object is at the end of the list.
Here's an example showing how you can utilize the 'Contains' method first before calling the 'LastIndexOf' method:

// check if the list contains the x before trying 'LastIndexOf'
if(arrList.Contains(x)){
    arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
}
else{
    // return -1 since the value doesn't exist in the List
    return -1;
}

This way, you minimize the number operations you perform on your list.

A: As of now, there seems no automatic tool available that performs LastIndexOf test with Pex automatically. Nevertheless, it can be written easily. This can be combined with the IndexOf test case for a complete coverage.
Here is an example of how you could write the LastIndexOf method test with Pex:
    [PexMethod]
    public void LastIndexOfTest(List<int> items, int value)
    {
        Assume.True(items is ArrayList); //This should be done to ensure ArrayList is--------------------            if (New_Ret == -1)
            {
                AssumePrecondition.IsFalse(New_arrListCount == -1);
            }

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ if x not in the list, the index returned should be -1
            AssumePrecondition.IsFalse(Old_arrListContainsX == false && New_arrListIndexOfX != -1);
        
	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ if x is in the list the returned index should not be -1, and it should be greater than the old last index of x
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(Old_arrListContainsX == true ? New_arrListIndexOfX != -1 && New_arrListIndexOfX > Old_arrListIndexOfX : true);

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  and @@@ new count is not changed
            Assert.IsTrue(Old_arrListCount == New_arrListCount);
        
	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ x should still be in the list
            Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListContainsX == Old_arrListContainsX && New_arrListContainsX == true);

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ the last index of x should be the value we have set
            Assert.IsTrue(Old_x == x && New_Ret == Old_arrListIndexOfX);
        
	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ last index must be greater than or equal to 0
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(New_Ret >= 0);
        
	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ last index cannot be more than list count
            AssumePrecondition.IsFalse(New_Ret > Old_arrListCount);
        }
    }
}

